  "summary": "I wrote the whole app in src/main.ts, but I could not run or verify any of it. `@aura3d/engine` is missing from node_modules in this sandbox, so `typecheck` reports \"Cannot find module '@aura3d/engine'\" and nothing from the library is type-checked. I didn't try `vite build` or a browser run either (no shell tool).\n\nI wrote the library calls from `llms.txt` and the docs, without checking them against real types. Treat the engine-facing parts as unverified.\n\n**What the app has:**\n- **Scene:** a glowing sun (emissive core, halo, point light, bloom) on a dark starfield of about 900 instanced emissive boxes in three tints. There are 8 planets, Mercury to Neptune, each with its own orbit radius, size, colour and speed (inner faster).\n- **Planet look:** each planet has a procedural vertex-colour texture (bands, noise, land and ocean, polar caps), and each spins on its own axis.\n- **Extras:** Saturn has a banded ring. Earth has one moon and Jupiter has two. Every planet has a thin circular orbit line.\n- **Camera:** it is fixed. I move the world instead, using nested groups for pitch, yaw and focus offset, because I couldn't confirm the camera-control API (`app.camera?.setPose`). Orbit-drag and scroll-zoom (25 to 150) work in the overview only.\n- **Focus:** clicking a planet, or calling `__focusPlanet(name)`, moves the camera over 1.5 s with cubic ease-in-out. It then follows the planet from the sun-facing side and shows a DOM panel with name, orbital period in Earth days and distance in million km. Clicking empty space, pressing Escape, or calling `__unfocus()` eases back to the overview and hides the panel.\n- **Picking:** it uses my own projection math. A planet's click radius is at least 10 px, so small planets are still easy to hit.\n- **Controls and state:** the Pause button switches to \"Play\" while paused. `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only getter with `ready`, `focused`, `paused`, `planets` and `cameraDistanceToFocus`. `__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frame.",
